  "query_preview": "..The 12Z KSLC sounding showed -11.5C at 700mb which\nis on the low end of the forecast envelope which is what led to\nthe widespread valley snow yesterday. This has also led to our\nfirst freezing temperatures of the season at KSLC which ties the\nlatest on record. Temperatures will remain 3-8 degrees below\nnormal through the next week. \n\nA transient ridge has moved overhead with mostly clear skies and\ncold temperatures, especially for areas that have snow covering\nthe ground. This will be a short-lived dry period before a broad\nslow moving trough pushes into the Great Basin tomorrow. This\ntrough will bring several rounds of precipitation through Saturday\nmorning. The first round moves in on Thursday with the heaviest\nacross southern Utah as an atmospheric river rounds the southern\nperiphery of the Sierras coinciding with the left exit region of a\n110+ kt jet. Snow levels will start much lower with this system\nwith snow levels generally between 6000-6500 feet. Flow aloft will\nstay southwesterly during the duration of this first wave which\nwill favor aspects that do well in southerly flow. \n\nThis first wave of precipitation associated with the initial \ntrough axis will exit by Friday afternoon. This will likely be the\nonly period of precipitation for the southern half of Utah as the\ntrailing wave Friday night into Saturday morning focuses on \nnorthern Utah/SW Wyoming. This limited duration across southern\nUtah will keep snow totals generally between 4-8\" for the southern\nmountains.\n\nA secondary trailing wave on Friday evening will usher in colder\nair aloft with median 700 mb temperatures falling to -10C which\nwould be cold enough to bring snow levels down to valley floors\nby Friday evening as winds aloft become west/northwesterly.\nGuidance is split on exactly how this will play out. A more\nwesterly flow will favor areas of Weber/Davis county, particularly\nthe benches while a more northwesterly flow will favor Tooele and\nSalt Lake valleys. Moisture will remain in the mid to lower\nportions of the column through 00Z Saturday before becoming\nconfined to the lower portion of the column, but this could allow\nsnow to continue through Saturday morning for areas across\nnorthern Utah that reside in the preferential flow regime. Snow\ntotals for the northern mountains will range from 10-20\" with\nhigher totals across the preferred aspects of 24\"+. Valley snow\ntotals will be between 1-4\" once a transition from rain to snow\noccurs.\n\n50% of guidance have another system moving through the state on\nMonday that could be cold enough for valley snow to accompany the\nentire event, but the other 50% of guidance do not bring this\nsystem through. Regardless, temperatures will be cold with the\nfirst freeze of the season likely for portions of Zion and lower\nWashington county Saturday and Sunday morning.",
